WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.310
So, in an ideal world, what would you hope to come out of the findings and the research and the new light shed on this?

2
00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:12.680
I think there are two things:

3
00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:34.380
one is a really positive and constructive collaboration between Georgetown and the Jesuits and the descendant community to help advance educational and economic opportunities for members of the descendant community who are in need.

4
00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:35.720
That’s one thing.

5
00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:41.960
The second thing is there’s a deeper question here: What is history good for?

6
00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:45.300
Why do we study history at all?

7
00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:51.520
Ultimately, I think one of the reasons we study history is to learn, but what do we learn?

8
00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:59.260
One thing we learn is that we need to try to broaden our moral imagination.

9
00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:00.880
So you look at what happened in 1838,

10
00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:19.360
and the Jesuits were operating within a very limited moral universe where they cared more about the financial stability of the university and the order than the lives of this group of people.

11
00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:22.473
They basically sacrificed that group of people for their own interest.

12
00:01:22.473 --> 00:01:28.800
If you look at the history like that, it’s a horrible history.

13
00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:33.000
A very traumatic history.

14
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:44.020
But rather than just say, “Those are bad people and they did a bad thing,”

15
00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:47.160
I think it should cause us to look at ourselves and think,

16
00:01:47.160 --> 00:02:04.340
“Well, what are we doing—who are we sacrificing for our own interests, and how can we look with new eyes at our own deeds?”

17
00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:14.360
So, I guess, ultimately, thinking and learning about this history should be able to make us more aware of our own morality.
